      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright© 2017, International Institute of Anticancer Research (Dr. George J. Delinasios), All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "Label": "BACKGROUND/AIM",
            "content": "Stage I splenic diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is rare and there are few data to guide management. We sought to further define prognosis and outcomes."
          },
          {
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "We utilized the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results registry to identify patients with stage I splenic DLBCL diagnosed 1973-2013. Patients were divided into two cohorts based on the year of diagnosis (1983-2005; 2006-2013) as rituximab was approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration in 2006 for first-line treatment of DLBCL."
          },
          {
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Utilization of splenectomy decreased after the approval of rituximab (82% pre- versus 72% rituximab-era). Disease-specific and overall survival were greater with splenectomy [hazard ratio (HR)=0.57, p=0.04; and HR=0.66, p=0.03, respectively], but this benefit was only seen in the pre-rituximab cohort, not in the rituximab-era cohort. There was a trend toward improved overall survival with the introduction of rituximab (HR=0.75, p=0.054)."
          },
          {
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Utilization of splenectomy for stage I splenic DLBCL has decreased with the introduction of rituximab without compromising outcomes."
          }
        ]
      },
